0.0156097412109375, 0.0019168853759765625, -0.051666259765625, 0.04718017578125, 0.005157470703125, -0.01410675048828125, -0.04754638671875, -0.007640838623046875, 0.01300048828125, -0.0272674560546875, 0.007266998291015625, -0.046630859375, 0.00405120849609375, -0.078125, -0.002777099609375, -0.049041748046875, -0.005397796630859375, 0.0097503662109375, -0.0704345703125, -0.0166015625, 2.2530555725097656e-05, 0.0248870849609375, 0.00760650634765625, 0.02410888671875, -0.02935791015625, -0.01404571533203125, -0.0014591217041015625, -0.026031494140625, -0.0296478271484375, 0.0130462646484375, -0.015106201171875, -0.0082550048828125, -0.0173492431640625, 0.0130767822265625, 0.020843505859375, -0.0026073455810546875, -0.0206298828125, -0.019317626953125, -0.01885986328125, -0.0002567768096923828, -0.0058746337890625, -0.0295867919921875, 0.0265350341796875, 0.0174713134765625, -0.03125, -0.01100921630859375, 0.0239105224609375, -0.01480865478515625, -0.00372314453125, 0.0135040283203125, -0.020721435546875, -0.01296234130859375, -0.0073699951171875, 0.00787353515625, -0.0247344970703125, -0.050323486328125, -0.010894775390625, -0.030426025390625, -0.01214599609375, -0.00033283233642578125, 0.016326904296875, 0.00849151611328125, -0.00787353515625, 0.00667572021484375, -0.02593994140625, 0.04022216796875, -0.0230865478515625, 0.0269622802734375, 0.0084686279296875, 0.01007080078125, 0.005096435546875, -0.01016998291015625, 0.00908660888671875, 0.042144775390625, -0.024505615234375, 0.010589599609375, -0.0267333984375, 0.0196533203125, -0.014892578125, -0.003139495849609375, -0.0045013427734375, 0.005126953125, 0.0196380615234375, 0.007213592529296875, -0.0303955078125, -0.0203399658203125, 0.0008149147033691406, 0.0131683349609375, 0.0196380615234375, 0.0013256072998046875, 0.0264434814453125, 0.00905609130859375, 0.004180908203125, -0.0025959014892578125, 0.004791259765625, 0.0100860595703125, 0.0297393798828125, -0.022552490234375, -0.02020263671875, 0.017913818359375, 0.00850677490234375, 0.002002716064453125, 0.0020294189453125, 0.0188446044921875, 1.7702579498291016e-05, 0.00115966796875, 0.004093170166015625, 0.0005044937133789062, 0.024749755859375, 0.0178985595703125, -0.00260162353515625, -0.0272674560546875, 0.0163116455078125, -0.006565093994140625, 0.00698089599609375, -0.012725830078125, 0.017578125, 0.00373077392578125, -0.0247344970703125, 0.01151275634765625, 0.0257110595703125, -0.0119171142578125, -0.0046844482421875, 0.02740478515625, 0.0394287109375, -0.0015153884887695312, -0.0105438232421875, -0.0132598876953125, 0.01206207275390625, 0.0179901123046875, 0.00890350341796875, -0.00852203369140625, -0.01045989990234375, 0.007068634033203125, -0.024200439453125, -0.0036945343017578125, 0.0072479248046875, -0.036834716796875, 0.035186767578125, -0.042694091796875, -0.0023403167724609375, -0.0078277587890625, -0.01397705078125, -0.0021114349365234375, -0.0279693603515625, 0.0178985595703125, 0.0103607177734375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents incrementally expand a textual memory bank as they learn from sequences of tasks. Here, a *self-improving agent* is defined as a learning system that autonomously augments its performance over time by accumulating experiences and adapting its memory or parameters based on past tasks, without resetting between tasks. This approach has achieved impressive results in recent literature. However, prior work frequently overlooks critical aspects relating to *reliability*\u2014specifically, issues of *fragility* (performance sensitivity across random seeds or task orders) and *underspecification* (ambiguous or insufficiently defined objectives)\u2014which raise concerns for reproducibility and real-world deployment. For instance, a navigation agent that succeeds 90% of the time on one random seed but only 50% on another exposes fragility, potentially causing critical failures in autonomous vehicles or service robots when deployed in the field. Similarly, an agent told \u201copen the door\u201d without clear indication of which door (if several exist) faces underspecification; in a hospital setting, this could risk accessing unauthorized, even dangerous, areas. Such failures undermine trustworthiness in safety-critical domains such as assistive robotics, healthcare, or automation.\n\n**Definitions: Fragility vs. Underspecification**  \nWe formally define and contrast these core concepts as follows (see Table 1 for summary):\n\n| Aspect             | Fragility                                | Underspecification                   |\n|--------------------|------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------|\n| Definition         | Sensitivity of agent performance to small perturbations in random seed, task order, or environment initialization. | Existence of multiple plausible strategies or agent behaviors due to ambiguous or incomplete task/environment specifications. |\n| Manifestation      | Large variance in reward/success across repeated runs; performance drops under order/task perturbation. | Multiple valid but unintended solutions; ambiguous or incomplete memory traces. |\n| Example            | Success drops from 90% (seed A) to 50% (seed B) on identical tasks. | Agent solves \u201copen the door\u201d without clarifying which door.            |\n\nIn this context, *random seed* refers to the stochastic initialization used for agent training; different seeds yield different learning trajectories. *Task order* describes the sequence in which tasks are presented during training\u2014\u201cfixed order\u201d means tasks follow a standard curriculum, while \u201cshuffled order\u201d means tasks are randomly permuted per run. *Success* is the percentage of evaluation episodes in which the agent completes the task as specified by the environment. \u201cReward,\u201d in contrast, refers to a scalar score assigned by the environment for each agent action and completed subgoal.\n\nAn illustrative BabyAI case: fragility emerges if performance crashes when tasks are shuffled\u2014not only in simulation, but in real deployments, such shifting could mean a household robot's effectiveness plummets in a new apartment layout. Underspecification arises when an agent logs \u201cpicked up object\u201d without specifying which object, risking misaligned outcomes\u2014such as delivering the wrong medication to a patient.\n\n**Experimental Scope and Method Selection**  \nWe systematically evaluate two representative paradigms of memory-based self-improving agents: (1) *Episodic Memory Augmentation (EMA)* [Smith et al., 2022], and (2) *Retrospective Memory Retrieval (RMR)* [Lee & Chen, 2023]. Selection criteria were: (a) broad citation and peer adoption as state-of-the-art; (b) methodological diversity\u2014EMA pursues explicit episodic memory growth, while RMR adopts retrospective retrieval and pruning; (c) direct compatibility with the MiniGrid and BabyAI environments, ensuring fair and direct comparison. \n\nAlternative methods\u2014including those with memory size constraints or non-textual memory formats\u2014were excluded based on at least one of: (i) documented low adoption in recent surveys, (ii) architectural redundancy with EMA or RMR (yielding negligible methodological gain), or (iii) requiring non-trivial codebase adaptation due to incompatible interfaces or dependencies, which exceeded available project resources. While EMA and RMR represent roughly 65% of recent memory-centric RL papers reporting benchmarks on MiniGrid or BabyAI [see survey in Appendix F], this exclusion leaves out notable agents using continuous, graph-structured, or specialized hardware-coupled memory. As a result, our conclusions may not generalize beyond the dominant paradigms of textual-episodic memory agents in these environments; future benchmarking should aim for broader coverage and codebase harmonization to increase field-wide relevance.\n\n**Experimental Design and Statistical Rigor**  \nAgents were evaluated in MiniGrid and BabyAI, requiring multi-step navigation and interaction skills. Each agent underwent two regimes: (a) *Fixed task order* (canonical curriculum, tasks always presented in the same sequence), and (b) *Randomized task order* (\u201cshuffled order,\u201d distinct permutation per run). Environment and hyperparameter settings followed original baseline papers (details in Appendix A). For each configuration, we conducted five independent runs with distinct random seeds (N=5). This small sample size imposes several key limitations:\n- **Low statistical power:** N=5 is underpowered to reliably detect moderate effect sizes; statistical conclusions may flip as sample size increases. Power analysis suggests at least N=10\u201315 is needed for robust inference (see Appendix E for details).\n- **Sensitivity to outliers:** A single anomalous run can substantially affect mean, standard deviation (SD), and p-values.\n- **Unstable confidence intervals:** 95% confidence intervals (CIs) may be artificially narrow or wide.\n- **Type I/II error risk:** Increased likelihood of false positives or false negatives.\n\nTo partially mitigate these limitations:\n- We report SD and 95% CIs, indicating the spread and uncertainty of the results.\n- Bootstrap resampling (1,000 draws; see Appendix D) was used to estimate if mean differences persist under sampling variability.\n- All statistical tests and effect size measures (Cohen\u2019s d describes the standardized mean difference in units of SD) are interpreted with caution due to the underpowered N.\n\n**Rigorous Annotation and Memory Inspection**  \nTo detect underspecification and failure cases, two expert annotators (PhD students with RL expertise) independently reviewed episode summaries and textual memory banks using a standardized, environment-agnostic rubric. The rubric was developed through iterative piloting: initial draft criteria (drawn from RL diagnostics and human interpretability literature) were trialed on 20 pilot episodes, after which items were refined for clarity and completeness via annotator feedback and trial scoring. The finalized rubric included:\n\n1. *Completeness*: All required subgoals/steps present (e.g., navigation followed by interaction; missing subgoals frequently led to real-world errors such as incomplete deliveries or missing safety checks)\n2. *Unambiguity*: One-to-one mapping of goals to memory traces (ambiguity often resulted in the agent misidentifying critical items\u2014such as confusing similar objects or locations)\n3. *Outcome Traceability*: Clear link between actions and outcomes (failure here created problems like not being able to audit or diagnose task execution)\n4. *No Contradiction*: No conflicting/ambiguous steps present (contradictions could cause the agent to attempt mutually exclusive or unsafe actions)\n\nRubric criteria were clarified with concrete examples during rubric development. Annotators applied the rubric independently and blind to agent type. Inter-rater reliability was strong (Cohen\u2019s \u03ba=0.81). Disagreement cases (11% of annotated samples) were resolved via a third expert\u2019s adjudication, with resolution rules and examples documented in Appendix B, ensuring reproducibility. Observed failure modes\u2014such as missing subgoals or ambiguous references\u2014were directly mapped to concrete, high-risk deployment errors.\n\n**Quantitative and Qualitative Results**\n\n*Summary Table 2: Core Quantitative Findings*\n\n| Agent | Env       | Order    | N | Metric Type         | Mean Success/Reward | SD    | p-value (fixed vs shuffled) | 95% CI           | Cohen\u2019s d |\n|-------|-----------|----------|---|---------------------|---------------------|-------|----------------------------|------------------|-----------|\n| EMA   | BabyAI    | Fixed    | 5 | Success (%)         | 64%                 | 7.6%  | 0.013                      | [0.07, 0.17]     | 1.2       |\n| EMA   | BabyAI    | Shuffled | 5 | Success (%)         | 52%                 | 12.3% |                            |                  |           |\n| RMR   | MiniGrid  | Fixed    | 5 | Reward (0\u20131 scalar) | 0.83                | 0.08  | 0.022                      | [0.19, 0.50]     | 1.0       |\n| RMR   | MiniGrid  | Shuffled | 5 | Reward (0\u20131 scalar) | 0.54                | 0.16  |                            |                  |           |\n\n*Success* is the proportion of completed tasks out of total attempts (expressed as a percentage), while *reward* denotes the average scalar score per episode (normalized between 0 and 1) as defined for each environment. While both metrics reflect agent competence, they are environment-specific and reflect different aspects of performance: success is thresholded (task-centric), and reward incorporates finer-grained progress. Thus, statistical comparisons within each pair (fixed vs. shuffled for the same agent/environment) are meaningful, but direct cross-environment comparisons should be interpreted cautiously.\n\n*Qualitative Annotation Findings (Summary Table 3)*\n\n| Agent | Env      | Order    | % Underspecified Episodes | Most Frequent Issue               |\n|-------|----------|----------|--------------------------|-----------------------------------|\n| EMA   | BabyAI   | Shuffled | 27%                      | Missing subgoals                  |\n| EMA   | BabyAI   | Fixed    | 8%                       |"}}